  "ingredients": "110g broken rice · 150g pork chops · 60g cucumber · 50g tomato · 40g carrots · 40g daikon · 30ml fish sauce · 30ml rice vinegar · 20g shallots · 20g sugar · 20ml sunflower oil · 15g lemongrass · 15g spring onion · 15ml honey · 10g garlic",
  "method": "⚖️ TẤM — the word means fragment — IS A MILLING DEFECT, AND THIS PLATE IS NAMED AFTER IT. This is rice that broke in the mill — snapped between the husking rollers and the polishing drum, sieved out of the whole grain because a broken kernel cannot be sold at grade. ⚖️ Every rice mill on earth produces it and every rice mill on earth sells it cheaper, because a buyer paying for length does not want halves. Saigon ate the halves, worked out that they were not worse but different, and built a plate around them that now costs more than the plate made with whole grain. ⛔ Do not treat this as an economy version of rice. It is a separate texture and the recipe is engineered for it.\n\n▶️ WHY THE FRAGMENT BEHAVES DIFFERENTLY. A whole grain of rice is a sealed parcel — a starchy centre wrapped in an intact outer layer that water has to work through slowly and evenly from the ends inward. ✅ Break that grain and you expose the interior directly on the fracture face, so water goes straight in. The result is a grain that hydrates faster, cooks in less time, and finishes softer and stickier than its whole equivalent because more starch has leached out of the open edges into the pot. ⚖️ That stickiness is the entire point: cơm tấm is eaten with a spoon and fork, not chopsticks, and it needs to hold together against the side of the spoon. Whole jasmine will not do that, which is why the substitution below is a compromise and the card says so plainly.\n\n⚠️ SOURCING IT HERE, HONESTLY. Broken jasmine rice turns up in Asian supermarkets and specialist online grocers and is worth seeking out, but it is not a supermarket staple in South Africa and you may well not find it. ✅ THE HONEST ROUTE: ordinary jasmine rice, pulsed in a blender in short bursts — three or four one-second pulses, checking between each. ⛔ Do not run the blender continuously; you want halves and thirds, not flour, and rice powder in the pot will turn the whole thing to paste. ⚠️ Expect it to be less even than the milled product, because a blade fractures randomly while a mill fractures along the grain's own weak line. Accept a mixed texture. It is still much closer than whole rice.\n\n▶️ WASH IT LESS THAN YOU THINK. ⚖️ The usual instruction to rinse rice until the water runs clear is written for whole grain, where the cloudiness is surface starch and dust from polishing. ⛔ Here a good part of that cloud is the grain's own interior, coming out of the fracture faces, and rinsing it away takes the stickiness with it. ✅ Two quick rinses, a swirl each, then stop while the water is still faintly milky.\n\n▶️ COOK IT DRIER. ✅ Use about one and a quarter parts water to one part rice by volume, against the one and a half you would use for whole jasmine. ⚖️ The fragments drink faster and hold less, so the ratio that gives you fluffy whole grain gives you porridge here. Bring it to a boil uncovered, drop to the lowest heat the plate will hold, lid on, twelve minutes, then off the heat with the lid still down for ten more. ⛔ Do not lift the lid during the rest — the last of the water is moving as steam through the bed and opening it drops the top layer back to raw.\n\n▶️ THE PORK IS MARINATED FOR SUGAR, NOT FOR TIME. Pound the lemongrass, garlic and shallots to a rough paste — a mortar, or the flat of a knife, or a short blitz. ✅ Mix with the fish sauce, sugar, honey and half the oil, and turn the chops through it for at least an hour, though overnight is better. ⚖️ THE SUGAR AND THE HONEY ARE STRUCTURAL. They are what let the surface caramelise and take colour in the few minutes the chop is over the fire, long before the interior would have browned on its own. Marinate in a mixture without sugar and you get a grey chop that is cooked through and looks boiled.\n\n▶️ GRILL IT OVER MODERATE HEAT AND WATCH IT LIKE A HAWK. ⚠️ That same sugar burns. Over a fierce fire the outside blackens bitter while the middle is still cold. ✅ Moderate coals or a medium pan, four to five minutes a side for a chop of about a centimetre and a half, turning two or three times rather than once. ⛔ A blackened chop is not char, it is a mistake, and no amount of sauce covers it. Rest it five minutes and slice against the grain.\n\n▶️ THE PICKLE AND THE SPRING ONION OIL DO THE BALANCING. Cut the carrot and daikon into fine matchsticks, toss with a pinch of salt, leave ten minutes, squeeze out the water, then cover with the rice vinegar and a spoon of sugar for at least twenty minutes. ✅ Meanwhile heat the rest of the oil until it just shimmers and pour it over the sliced spring onion in a small bowl. ⚖️ The oil must be hot enough to hiss and no hotter — you are cooking the onion in the bowl, not frying it, and oil that is smoking turns it bitter and dull green in seconds.\n\n▶️ BUILD THE PLATE, DO NOT MIX IT. Rice down first and pressed lightly flat, sliced pork laid over one half, the pickle and the sliced cucumber and tomato on the other, and the spring onion oil spooned over the rice rather than the meat. ⚖️ Cơm tấm is a plate of separate things that meet on the spoon, and each spoonful is assembled by the eater. ⛔ Stirring it together is not a shortcut to the same dish, it is a different and worse one.",
