  "ingredients": "1 baguette · 50g ham · 40g chicken livers · 40g carrots · 40g daikon · 40g cucumber · 30g butter · 30ml rice vinegar · 25g mayonnaise · 20g sugar · 10g coriander · 10ml soy sauce · 5g chilli · salt",
  "method": "MOST SANDWICHES FAIL ON THE BREAD BEING TOO GOOD. ⚖️ That sounds backwards, so here is the arithmetic. A proper French baguette is roughly a third crust by weight, dense-crumbed and chewy, and it was designed to be the substantial part of a meal — bread with something on it. Bánh mì inverts that. The filling is the meal and the bread is the wrapper, so a Vietnamese loaf is baked to be as close to empty as a loaf can be: a crust thin enough to shatter under your thumb, and a crumb so open it is mostly air. ✅ THE TARGET RATIO IS ROUGHLY ONE PART BREAD TO TWO PARTS FILLING BY WEIGHT, and a dense loaf makes that impossible because the bread alone fills the roll.\n\n▶️ WHY THE VIETNAMESE LOAF IS LIGHTER, AND WHAT TO BUY HERE. Vietnamese bakeries cut the wheat flour with a proportion of rice flour, which brings less protein to the dough and therefore builds a weaker gluten network. ⚖️ A weaker network cannot hold large bubbles under tension, so it blows out into many small ones and then sets thin and brittle rather than chewy and elastic. That is the whole difference in one sentence. ⚠️ You will not find that loaf in a South African supermarket and this card is not going to pretend otherwise. ✅ THE HONEST ROUTE: a Portuguese roll or a soft-crusted small baguette from a supermarket bakery, never an artisan sourdough and never a ciabatta. ⛔ A chewy, well-developed crust is a genuine defeat here — you will pull the filling out with your first bite and be left holding bread.\n\n✅ HOLLOW THE ROLL IF IT IS DENSE, AND DO NOT APOLOGISE. Split it along one side rather than all the way through, then pull out a good handful of the soft interior with your fingers. ⚖️ This is standard practice in Saigon with a heavier loaf and it is the single most effective fix available to you here. You are manufacturing the airiness the baking did not give you. The pulled crumb is not waste — dry it and it is breadcrumbs.\n\n▶️ MAKE THE ĐỒ CHUA FIRST, BECAUSE IT NEEDS THE HOUR. Đồ chua — the pickled carrot and daikon that every bánh mì carries — is cut into fine matchsticks, salted for ten minutes, squeezed hard, and then covered with the rice vinegar and sugar for at least an hour. ⚖️ The salt-and-squeeze is not optional and it is not just about water: it collapses the cell walls enough that the vinegar goes in properly, so an unsalted pickle tastes of vinegar on the outside and raw vegetable in the middle. ✅ It keeps two weeks and is better on day three.\n\n▶️ THE PÂTÉ AND THE MAYONNAISE ARE ONE LAYER, NOT TWO CONDIMENTS. Soften the chicken livers in a pan with a little butter until just cooked through and still faintly pink inside, then blend with the rest of the butter and a good pinch of salt until completely smooth and cool it. ⚖️ Pâté on one cut face, mayonnaise on the other, both spread properly to the edges. ⛔ Do not skip either and do not use only one. They are not the same thing doing the same job — the pâté brings iron-rich savour and the mayonnaise brings acid and softness, and a roll with only mayonnaise tastes thin while one with only pâté tastes heavy and slightly metallic.\n\n▶️ SEASON THE INSIDE OF THE BREAD, NOT THE FILLING. A short splash of soy sauce straight onto the cut faces before anything else goes in. ⚖️ In Vietnam this is a specific seasoning sauce and soy is the closest thing on a South African shelf. ⚠️ A splash, not a pour — the bread is a sponge and too much makes the whole base soggy before it reaches the table.\n\n▶️ BUILD IT IN THE RIGHT ORDER OR IT WILL COLLAPSE. Pâté and mayonnaise, then the soy, then the ham, then the đồ chua squeezed of its liquid, then cucumber, then whole coriander sprigs and sliced chilli on top. ⚖️ THE WET THINGS GO IN THE MIDDLE, WITH FAT ABOVE AND BELOW THEM. The pâté and mayonnaise layers are effectively waterproofing the crumb from both faces, so the pickle can sit against them without soaking through. ⛔ Pickle laid directly on bare bread will have softened it within twenty minutes.\n\n✅ SQUEEZE THE PICKLE, ALWAYS. Lift it out with a fork and press it against the side of the jar. ⚠️ Carrying the vinegar across into the roll is the difference between a sandwich that survives a lunchbox and one that does not.\n\n▶️ EAT IT WITHIN THE HOUR, OR PACK IT IN PARTS. ⚖️ Nothing about this construction is designed for keeping. If it is going into a bag for later, wrap the pickle separately and assemble at the other end — it takes thirty seconds and it is the difference between the sandwich you intended and a damp one.",
