  "trivia": "The loaf arrived with a colonial administration and then quietly changed species. France brought the baguette to Indochina in the nineteenth century as an imported luxury eaten by administrators, made with imported wheat, and for decades it stayed that: a European object in a Vietnamese city. What turned it into something else was cost. Wheat had to be shipped and was expensive, rice was local and cheap, and bakers began cutting the flour to make the loaf affordable. The substitution was economic and the consequence was textural — less wheat protein means less gluten, less gluten means a weaker structure, and a weaker structure means a loaf that goes light and brittle instead of chewy and dense. Bakers who had been failing to reproduce a French baguette had accidentally produced a better sandwich roll, because a nearly hollow loaf can hold twice its own weight in filling without becoming bread-heavy. The sandwich as it is now eaten did not appear until the 1950s, in Saigon, when the pâté and mayonnaise of the colonial larder met the pickled vegetables, coriander and chilli of the Vietnamese table in the same roll. It is one of the few dishes in the world where you can point at the exact structural property that made it possible and name the economic reason it exists.",
