  "ingredients": "100g glutinous rice · 50g mung beans · 25g shallots · 30ml sunflower oil · 5g turmeric · salt",
  "method": "NOTHING HERE IS COOKED IN WATER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. ⚖️ Xôi is street food eaten out of a leaf or a scrap of paper, in the hand, walking. It has to hold together as a block, stay warm for an hour, and never leave a wet mark on the paper. Every instruction below exists to serve that, and the two that matter most are that the rice is soaked in cold water overnight and then steamed over water rather than boiled in it.\n\n▶️ SOAK OVERNIGHT, AND UNDERSTAND WHY IT IS NOT OPTIONAL. Glutinous rice in a steamer only ever meets water as vapour, and vapour cannot carry enough liquid into a dry grain to hydrate it through — you would get a soft outside and a chalky, opaque centre no matter how long you steamed it. ✅ So the water goes in first, cold, over six to eight hours, and the steam's only job afterwards is heat. ⛔ Six hours is the floor. A ninety-minute soak in warm water is the internet's shortcut and it produces exactly the chalky centre described above.\n\n▶️ TURMERIC GOES INTO THE SOAK, NOT THE STEAMER. Stir the turmeric through the soaking water at the start. ⚖️ It is doing colour work rather than flavour work here — a whisper of earthiness at most — and it needs the full soak to penetrate, because sprinkling it on at the end colours the outside of the grains and leaves the interiors white. ✅ Salt goes in at the same time and for the same reason.\n\n▶️ DRAIN IT PROPERLY, THEN STEAM IT DRY. Tip the soaked rice into a sieve and leave it a full ten minutes to stop dripping. ⚠️ Rice carrying loose surface water into the steamer is the commonest reason xôi comes out gluey rather than distinct. Spread it in an even layer no more than four centimetres deep, make a few holes through it with the handle of a spoon so steam can climb, and steam for 25 to 30 minutes. ✅ It is done when a grain is translucent all the way through and squashes without any chalk in the middle.\n\n▶️ THE MUNG BEAN IS THE PART EVERYONE GETS WRONG. Soak the split mung beans separately for four hours, drain, and steam them alongside or below the rice for about 25 minutes until completely soft. ⚖️ NOW MASH THEM HOT AND PRESS THEM HARD. While still steaming, work them to a smooth paste with a pinch of salt, then pack that paste tightly into a bowl or a mug and press it down with real force until it is a solid, dense block with no air in it. Turn it out and let it cool and firm.\n\n⚖️ XÉO MEANS TO SLICE ON THE DIAGONAL, AND THAT IS THE NAME OF THE DISH. The block is not spooned over the rice. It is shaved — held in one hand and cut down its face with a knife or the edge of a spoon so that thin ribbons fall over the top in a loose pile. ✅ THIS IS NOT PRESENTATION, IT IS TEXTURE. A spoonful of mung bean paste is a wet lump that sits on the rice and gets mixed into it. A shaved ribbon is a dry, crumbly sheet that lies across the surface, keeps its own identity in the mouth, and is still recognisably separate on the fourth mouthful. ⛔ Skip the pressing and there is nothing solid enough to shave, which is why the pressing and the shaving are one instruction.\n\n▶️ FRY THE SHALLOTS SLOWLY AND PULL THEM EARLY. Slice them as thinly as you can and fry in the oil over a medium-low heat, moving them constantly, until they are an even pale gold. ⚠️ Lift them out onto paper while they still look slightly underdone — they carry on colouring hard off the heat, and shallots taken to brown in the pan will be bitter on the plate. ✅ Keep the oil. It is now shallot-flavoured and a spoonful of it goes over the rice.\n\n▶️ ASSEMBLE IN THREE LAYERS AND DO NOT STIR. Warm rice pressed lightly flat, a spoon of the shallot oil over it, then the shaved mung bean, then the fried shallots. ⚖️ Three dry things stacked, no sauce anywhere, which is exactly why it travels. ⛔ Stirring it together gives you a yellow-brown mush that tastes of the same ingredients and is a genuinely worse dish.",
  "cookTime": "Soak 8 hr · Prep 15 min · Cook 30 min",
  "kcal": "~430 kcal",
  "nutrition": "Protein 12g · Carbs 74g · Fat 11g · Sodium ~320mg",
  "storage": "Best warm on the day. Keeps 2 days covered in the fridge and must be steamed, never microwaved — microwaving drives water out of the grains unevenly and leaves hard patches. ✅ The pressed mung bean block keeps 3 days and is easier to shave cold than warm. ⚠️ Fried shallots go soft in the fridge within hours; store them in a jar at room temperature and they stay crisp for a week.",
  "chefNotes": "⚖️ The soak is the recipe. Everything else is assembly, and no amount of extra steaming rescues rice that went in dry.\n\n✅ Press the mung bean harder than feels reasonable. If it crumbles when you try to shave it, it was not packed tightly enough — mash it again while warm and press it properly.\n\n⚠️ Split mung beans and whole green mung beans are not interchangeable here. The whole ones keep their skins and will not mash smooth. If only whole ones are available, soak longer and rub the skins off after soaking.\n\n⛔ Do not salt the mung bean and the rice at the same rate. The rice carries most of the seasoning; the bean layer should taste mild, or the plate reads as salty all the way through with nothing to rest against.",
  "pairsWith": "Alongside Cơm Tấm · Strong coffee · A plain omelette",
  "goesWith": "Extra fried shallots · A little more shallot oil · Chilli salt on the side",
  "trivia": "The mung bean has been quietly doing structural work in Asian kitchens for far longer than it has been doing flavour work, and xôi xéo is one of the clearest cases. A pulse that is cooked, mashed and then compressed behaves less like a vegetable and more like a soft cheese: the starch granules, already burst by cooking, pack together as the mass cools and set into something that holds a cut face and takes a blade cleanly. That is the same physical principle behind pressed bean curd and behind the moulded bean pastes used across the region in sweets, and it is why a warm paste and a cooled pressed block of the identical mixture eat so differently. The word xéo, meaning to slice across at an angle, describes the vendor's actual working motion — the block held against the palm and shaved with a thin blade in the same movement a cook uses to shave chocolate. It has become the name of the dish, which tells you the trade regarded the cut, rather than the ingredients, as the thing worth naming. Vietnam has dozens of xôi variants and almost all of them are named for what is on top; this is the one named for how it got there.",
  "howThisFeels": "Warm, dry and portable — the yellow block shaved over the top in ribbons, eaten out of paper with no cutlery in sight.",
