  "ingredients": "60g rice flour · 60g prawns · 50g pork belly · 60g bean sprouts · 50g lettuce · 100ml coconut milk · 30ml sunflower oil · 20g spring onion · 20ml fish sauce · 15g limes · 10g mint · 10g coriander · 10g sugar · 5g garlic · 5g chilli · 3g turmeric · salt",
  "method": "A LID IS THE ENTIRE RECIPE, AND KNOWING WHEN TO TAKE IT OFF IS THE SKILL. ⚖️ Xèo is the noise — the hiss the batter makes hitting hot fat, and the dish is named after a sound rather than an ingredient, which tells you the pan is doing the work. What comes out has to be two things at once: an edge so thin and brittle it snaps, and a centre soft enough to hold a filling and fold without cracking. ✅ One batter produces both, and the only thing that decides which part gets which is whether the lid is on.\n\n▶️ HOW ONE BATTER MAKES TWO TEXTURES. When the batter goes in, you swirl the pan so it runs thin at the rim and pools slightly in the middle. Lid on, and the pan fills with steam: the centre, which is deeper and wetter, cooks through gently in that damp heat and stays pliable. ⚖️ Lid off, and the steam escapes in seconds — now the thin rim, which has almost no water left in it, is sitting on hot oil in dry air and it fries. ✅ STEAM COOKS THE MIDDLE, DRY HEAT CRISPS THE EDGE, AND THE LID IS THE SWITCH BETWEEN THEM. ⛔ Leave the lid on throughout and the whole thing is a soft pancake, because the water never gets a chance to leave. Never put it on at all and the middle is raw where the filling sits.\n\n▶️ THE BATTER IS RICE FLOUR AND COCONUT MILK, AND IT MUST REST. Whisk the rice flour, coconut milk, turmeric, a pinch of salt and about 100ml of water until completely smooth, then leave it at least thirty minutes. ⚖️ Rice flour is milled far coarser than wheat flour and its particles need time to take up water — a batter used straight away cooks grainy and tears when you fold it. ✅ It should end up the consistency of thin cream. If it has thickened while resting, loosen it with water; a thick batter cannot run thin at the rim, and without a thin rim there is nothing to crisp.\n\n⚠️ THE TURMERIC IS FOR COLOUR, AND THREE GRAMS IS PLENTY. It is what makes the crepe yellow, and it contributes very little taste at this quantity. ⛔ A heavy hand turns the batter dull orange and faintly medicinal, which is the commonest way this dish is ruined before the pan is even hot.\n\n▶️ COOK THE FILLING IN THE PAN FIRST AND LEAVE IT THERE. Slice the pork belly thin and fry it hard in a little of the oil until the fat has rendered and the edges have caught. Add the prawns and the sliced spring onion and cook a minute more. ✅ Spread it out across the pan and leave it in place — the batter goes straight over the top of it, so the filling is embedded in the crepe rather than spooned onto it afterwards.\n\n▶️ THE PAN MUST BE HOTTER THAN FEELS SENSIBLE, AND THE OIL GENEROUS. Get it properly hot, add the rest of the oil, and pour the batter into the centre from a height while tilting and swirling the pan in one continuous movement. ⚠️ You have about two seconds before it sets, which is why the swirl has to be one movement and not three. A pan that is merely warm gives you a batter that sits in a puddle instead of running, and no hiss — if it does not make the noise, the pan was not ready.\n\n▶️ NOW THE SEQUENCE THAT MATTERS. Bean sprouts scattered over one half, lid on, ninety seconds. ✅ Lid off, and now leave it alone on the heat for another two to three minutes while the edge goes from pale to deep gold and starts lifting away from the pan by itself. ⚖️ That lifting is the signal rather than the colour, so the pan tells you before your eyes do — a crepe that releases on its own is crisp, and one you have to prise off is not ready. Fold it in half over the sprouts and slide it out.\n\n▶️ IT IS NOT EATEN WITH CUTLERY, AND THAT CHANGES HOW YOU CUT IT. Break the folded crepe into rough pieces, wrap each piece in a lettuce leaf with whole mint and coriander, and dip it in nước chấm. ⚖️ THE LETTUCE IS STRUCTURAL — it is the only thing stopping a shard of crisp crepe from cutting the roof of your mouth, and it is why the herbs are whole leaves rather than chopped. ⛔ Do not stack finished crepes on top of each other while you cook the rest; they steam themselves soft in about a minute. Onto a rack, or straight to the table.\n\n▶️ MAKE THE NƯỚC CHẤM WHILE THE BATTER RESTS. Fish sauce, lime juice, sugar, crushed garlic and chopped chilli into about 60ml of warm water, whisked until the sugar goes. ✅ Taste it against a piece of plain lettuce, not off a spoon — it is dressing something rich and salty and it should read slightly sharp and slightly weak on its own.",
  "cookTime": "Prep 20 min · Rest 30 min · Cook 20 min",
  "kcal": "~560 kcal",
  "nutrition": "Protein 26g · Carbs 48g · Fat 30g · Sodium ~1350mg",
  "storage": "⛔ This does not keep and there is no honest way to say otherwise — a bánh xèo is crisp for about four minutes and soft forever after. Batter keeps 2 days in the fridge and is arguably better on day two; whisk it and check the consistency before using. Nước chấm keeps a week. Cook the crepes to order.",
  "chefNotes": "⚖️ Lid on for the middle, lid off for the edge. If you remember nothing else from this card, remember that it is a two-stage cook in one pan.\n\n✅ Thin batter, hot pan, generous oil. All three, or the edge will not crisp. This is not a dish for a dry non-stick pan and a teaspoon of oil.\n\n⚠️ A 20cm pan is far easier than a 28cm one. A large crepe is thicker at the rim by the time the batter has run that far, and it tears when you fold it.\n\n⛔ Do not skip the rest. Thirty minutes is the minimum. Under that, the crepe cooks gritty and tears at the fold.",
  "pairsWith": "Gỏi Cuốn alongside · Cold beer · Chè Chuối after",
  "goesWith": "A big plate of lettuce and herbs · Extra nước chấm · Sliced chilli",
  "trivia": "The batter is a rare case of a cuisine solving a problem it did not know it had. Wheat crepes get their flexibility from gluten — a protein network that stretches, holds gas and lets a thin sheet be lifted, folded and rolled without breaking. Rice flour contains no gluten at all, so by every expectation a rice-flour crepe should be a fragile disc that shatters when touched. It does not, and the reason is that rice starch behaves very differently from wheat starch once it has absorbed water and been heated: the granules swell and burst, and the released starch sets on cooling into a continuous film that is genuinely elastic while still warm. The structure comes from starch rather than protein, and it is temperature-dependent rather than mechanical — which is exactly why a bánh xèo folds beautifully in the pan and cracks if you try to fold it ten minutes later. The coconut milk is doing a second job in the same direction. Fat coats starch granules and physically interferes with them bonding to each other, so a batter enriched with coconut milk sets into a slightly looser, more open film that traps steam as it fries. Those trapped pockets are what make the edge shatter rather than merely harden. The name is onomatopoeic across a whole family of dishes in the region, and it is worth noticing that a cuisine chose to name this one after the sound of the fat rather than after anything in it.",
  "howThisFeels": "That noise the dish is named for, then a folded gold half-moon that shatters into a lettuce leaf.",
