  "ingredients": "25g coffee · 30g condensed milk · 10g sugar · salt",
  "method": "THIS IS BUILT ON A BEAN THAT THE REST OF THE COFFEE WORLD SPENT A CENTURY CALLING INFERIOR. ⚖️ Almost every coffee you have been sold as good is arabica. Vietnam grows overwhelmingly robusta, and robusta is a genuinely different species with a genuinely different chemistry: roughly twice the caffeine, markedly less sugar and less of the aromatic oil that gives arabica its florality, and considerably more of the bitter compounds. ✅ Brewed the way you would brew arabica it tastes harsh and flat, which is precisely why it is cheap. Brewed the Vietnamese way and sweetened the Vietnamese way it tastes like nothing else, and the method below is not a quirk — it is the correct answer to that specific bean.\n\n▶️ GRIND MEDIUM-COARSE, NOT FINE. ⚖️ The instinct with a strong drink is to grind fine, and here it is wrong. A phin — the small metal filter that sits on the glass — has no pump and no pressure behind it, only gravity. ⛔ Fine grounds pack down under the weight of the water and choke it, which is why a phin that stops dripping cannot be rescued. ✅ Aim for something like coarse sand. If yours has stalled, the grind was too fine or the press was screwed down too hard.\n\n▶️ THE PHIN IS NOT REALLY A DRIP BREWER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT. In a paper-filter brewer, water passes through the bed and leaves. In a phin, water sits on top of a restricted plate and works its way down over four or five minutes, so the grounds spend that entire time submerged. ⚖️ IT IS AN IMMERSION BREW WEARING A DRIP BREWER'S CLOTHES, and immersion extracts far more than a fast pour does. That is how you get something close to syrup out of a filter with no pressure anywhere in it. ✅ FOUR TO FIVE MINUTES IS THE TARGET. Under two and it is weak and sour; over eight and the bitterness has gone woody.\n\n⚠️ IF YOU HAVE NO PHIN, USE A CAFETIÈRE AND ACCEPT THE DIFFERENCE. Same grind, a quarter of the usual water, five minutes, pressed slowly. ⚖️ It is genuinely close, because a cafetière is also an immersion brew — much closer than any machine that forces water through under pressure. ⛔ Do not use an espresso machine. It is built for the opposite problem and gives you 25ml of something bitter and thin.\n\n▶️ BLOOM IT FIRST, WITH BARELY ANY WATER. Grounds into the phin, plate on top, and about 20ml of hot water. Wait thirty seconds and watch it swell. ✅ Fresh grounds hold carbon dioxide from roasting, and gas leaving the bed pushes water out of its way — pour everything at once and the first minute of extraction is fighting bubbles instead of coffee. ⚠️ Water just off the boil, not boiling, otherwise the bitterness arrives early and stays.\n\n▶️ THE CONDENSED MILK GOES IN THE GLASS FIRST, UNDERNEATH. ⚖️ It is far denser than the coffee and sits as a distinct layer at the bottom, which is deliberate — the drink is served unstirred and stirred at the table, so you see the two layers before you wreck them. ✅ Fill the phin with the rest of the water, and let it drip straight onto that layer.\n\n⚖️ THE SWEETNESS IS DOING CHEMISTRY, NOT INDULGENCE. Sugar suppresses the perception of bitterness directly — it does not mask it, it measurably reduces how bitter the same liquid reads. ✅ With a bean carrying this much bitter compound, that is the difference between a drink and a punishment. ⚠️ A pinch of salt does the same job from the other side and more efficiently than sugar does; a few grains in the glass is standard practice and it is worth doing.\n\n▶️ ICE LAST, AND ONLY WHEN THE BREWING HAS FINISHED. Stir the coffee and milk together thoroughly first, then pour the lot over a full glass of ice. ⛔ Do not brew onto ice. The coffee needs to be hot enough to dissolve the condensed milk properly, and milk hitting ice-cold coffee stays in ropey streaks that never fully combine. ✅ Fill the glass with ice — a half-filled glass melts fast and gives you a weak drink, so the packed glass is not generosity, it is what holds the strength.",
  "cookTime": "Prep 5 min · Brew 5 min",
  "kcal": "~180 kcal",
  "nutrition": "Protein 3g · Carbs 30g · Fat 5g · Sodium ~60mg",
  "storage": "⛔ Drink it now. Coffee over ice is at its best for about ten minutes and the melt only goes one way. ✅ The brewed coffee alone keeps 2 days in the fridge and is genuinely useful — brew a batch, keep it, and build a glass in thirty seconds. Condensed milk keeps a month in the fridge once opened, in a jar rather than the tin.",
  "chefNotes": "⚖️ Robusta, not arabica. If your shop sells a Vietnamese-style ground coffee it is almost certainly robusta or a robusta-heavy blend, and it is the right thing to buy.\n\n✅ Coarser grind than feels right, and never screw the phin press down tight. Rest it on the bed and leave it.\n\n⚠️ Condensed milk is not evaporated milk and the two are not interchangeable — evaporated is unsweetened and will give you a thin, grey, faintly savoury drink.\n\n⛔ Do not stir the phin while it drips. Every stir disturbs the bed and channels the water, and the second half of the brew comes through under-extracted.",
  "pairsWith": "After Bánh Mì · Alongside Xôi Xéo at breakfast · With anything from the dessert shelf",
  "goesWith": "A glass of plain iced tea on the side · Extra condensed milk for those who want it",
  "trivia": "Vietnam is the second largest coffee producer on earth and grows almost none of what the speciality trade considers good coffee, and both of those facts come from the same colonial decision. The French planted coffee in the highlands in the nineteenth century, and arabica struggled — it wants cool nights and high altitude and it is notoriously vulnerable to leaf rust, which moves fast in a warm wet climate. Robusta shrugged all of it off. It tolerates heat, resists the rust that was destroying arabica plantations across the tropics, and yields far more per tree. So the country ended up committed to a bean that the export market prices as a commodity for instant coffee and blending, rather than as something to be drunk on its own. Vietnamese coffee culture then did the thing nobody expected, because the alternative was to keep failing at somebody else's standard: it built a preparation around robusta's actual properties rather than around arabica's. The long immersion suits a bean with less delicate aromatics to lose, because there is nothing fragile left to overextract. The heavy sweetening answers a bean with more bitterness to manage, which is why the sugar is not optional here in the way it is with arabica. The condensed milk arrived because fresh dairy in a hot country before reliable refrigeration was a genuine problem and a tin was not, and it stayed long after refrigeration solved that, because by then it was the taste people wanted. The result is a national drink that would be actively worse if it were made with the expensive bean.",
  "howThisFeels": "Two layers in a glass, pale underneath and almost black above, until somebody stirs and it all goes the colour of caramel.",
