  "ingredients": "150g hake · 120g pineapple · 100g tomatoes · 60g okra · 60g bean sprouts · 50g celery · 30ml fish sauce · 20g tamarind paste · 20g sugar · 15g spring onion · 10g coriander · 10g garlic · 5g chilli · salt",
  "method": "CANH IS NOT A STARTER AND IT IS NOT A COURSE, WHICH IS THE ONE THING WORTH UNDERSTANDING BEFORE YOU COOK IT. ⚖️ A Vietnamese family meal arrives all at once: rice, something grilled or braised and salty, something green, and a bowl of canh in the middle that everybody spoons from throughout. It is not eaten first and it is not eaten alone. Its job is mechanical — rice is dry, the salty dish is intense, and the soup is what carries both down and resets the mouth between them. ✅ THAT IS WHY IT IS THIN, SOUR AND UNDER-SEASONED BY THE STANDARDS OF A SOUP YOU WOULD SERVE ON ITS OWN. ⛔ Cook it as a rich standalone soup and it stops doing the job it exists for.\n\n▶️ SO SEASON IT AGAINST THE RICE, NOT AGAINST YOUR SPOON. Taste it, then taste it again with a mouthful of plain rice. ⚖️ On its own it should read slightly too sour and slightly too weak. Against rice it comes right. A canh that tastes perfect from the spoon will taste heavy and salty the moment it meets the meal it is part of.\n\n▶️ THE SOURNESS IS TAMARIND, AND IT BEHAVES DIFFERENTLY FROM VINEGAR OR CITRUS. Loosen the tamarind paste in a little warm water and strain it in. ⚖️ Tamarind carries tartaric acid along with a good deal of sugar and a genuine fruit body, so it sours without the sharp volatile edge of vinegar and without the perfume of lime. ✅ It also holds up to heat, whereas lime juice added early cooks off to a flat dullness. That is why the tamarind goes in at the start and any lime, if you use it, goes in at the very end.\n\n▶️ PINEAPPLE IN A SAVOURY BROTH IS NOT A SWEET FLOURISH. Cut it into chunks and add it early with the tomatoes. ⚖️ It is doing two jobs at once: contributing acid of a completely different character from the tamarind, and contributing sugar that keeps that combined acidity from reading as harsh. ⚠️ A canh chua with tamarind and no pineapple tastes thin and one-dimensionally sour, which is the commonest way this soup disappoints.\n\n⚠️ THE VEGETABLE THAT IS NOT AVAILABLE HERE, SAID PLAINLY. The traditional stem is bạc hà — elephant ear stem — which is spongy and absorbs broth like nothing else. It is not sold in South Africa and there is no close substitute. ✅ Celery is the honest stand-in: it brings the same fresh snap and the same faint bitterness, and it is what this card is built on. ⛔ It does not soak up broth the way bạc hà does, so accept that the texture is different rather than pretending otherwise.\n\n▶️ THE OKRA IS THERE FOR BODY, AND HOW YOU CUT IT DECIDES HOW MUCH. Cut it into thick pieces on the diagonal and add it near the end. ⚖️ Okra releases a thickening substance from its cut faces, and more cut surface means more of it. Thick pieces added late give the broth a very slight weight without turning it ropey. ⛔ Sliced thin and simmered long, it will make the soup slippery, and there is no recovering a canh that has gone that way.\n\n▶️ BUILD THE BROTH FIRST AND KEEP IT MOVING GENTLY. Bring about 700ml of water to a simmer with the tamarind liquid, the fish sauce, the sugar and a pinch of salt. Add the pineapple and tomatoes and give them five minutes. ✅ Then the celery, then the okra, two minutes apart. ⚠️ Never let it boil hard at any point — a rolling boil breaks the tomatoes down into the broth and clouds it, and canh chua should be clear enough to see the fish through.\n\n▶️ THE FISH GOES IN LAST AND BARELY COOKS. Cut the hake into large pieces, slide them in, and take the pot off the heat within three minutes. ⚖️ The residual heat finishes them while you are carrying the pot to the table. ⛔ Fish simmered in a sour broth firms up and goes cottony fast, and the acid speeds that up rather than slowing it — this is the opposite of a braise and it wants a light hand.\n\n▶️ THE BEAN SPROUTS AND HERBS NEVER TOUCH THE HEAT. Raw sprouts, sliced spring onion, coriander and chilli into the serving bowl, and the hot soup poured over. ✅ They wilt just enough and keep their snap. ⛔ Sprouts cooked in the pot go limp and slightly sulphurous within a minute.",
  "cookTime": "Prep 15 min · Cook 20 min",
