  "nutrition": "Protein 30g · Carbs 26g · Fat 3g · Sodium ~1500mg",
  "storage": "Keeps 2 days in the fridge, but take the fish out and store it separately — reheating it in the broth is what turns it woolly. ✅ The broth alone is genuinely better on day two, once the tamarind and pineapple have settled into each other. Reheat to a bare simmer and add fresh sprouts and herbs at the table, never the reheated ones.",
  "chefNotes": "⚖️ Under-season it. This is the one soup on the collection where tasting it alone and adjusting until it is delicious will actively make it worse in its actual setting.\n\n✅ Tamarind and pineapple together, always. They are two different acids doing two different things and neither works alone here.\n\n⚠️ Tamarind pastes vary hugely in strength and in how much sugar they carry. Start with half of what is listed, taste, and climb.\n\n⛔ Do not boil it hard, and do not cook the fish beyond three minutes. Both faults are irreversible and both are common.",
  "pairsWith": "With Cơm Tấm and rice · Alongside any grilled dish · Xôi Xéo for a bigger table",
  "goesWith": "A bowl of plain rice · Extra chilli · A saucer of fish sauce for dipping the fish",
  "trivia": "Almost every food culture that eats a dry staple has independently arrived at a thin liquid to eat beside it, and the interesting thing is how rarely that liquid is treated as a dish in its own right. Vietnamese canh, Korean guk, Japanese suimono and a good deal of Chinese everyday soup all share the same structural position: served with the meal rather than before it, spooned between mouthfuls, and deliberately kept light because a rich soup would compete with the dishes it is meant to support. The word canh simply means soup in the everyday sense, and canh chua specifies that this one is the sour kind, which tells you the sourness is the distinguishing feature rather than the fish. That distinction matters in the south, where the dish belongs. The Mekong Delta is hot and humid for most of the year, and a sour, cool-tasting broth on the table does something a rich one cannot — acidity stimulates saliva, which is precisely what a heavy salty meal in high heat suppresses. It is a piece of practical engineering that got repeated until it became a taste, and the same logic sits behind sour soups in Thailand, the Philippines and across the region. Southern Vietnamese cooking is also markedly sweeter than northern, and this soup is a clear case: a northern cook would use noticeably less sugar and would probably be faintly disapproving about the pineapple.",
  "howThisFeels": "A clear sour broth in the middle of the table that everybody keeps reaching back into without ever really talking about it.",
